Verbatim depends on third-party software. Nothing is vendored: every dependency
is fetched by pip or npm at install time, under its own licence. This file
records the ones whose licences ask to be named. It is not a complete
dependency list, which pip and npm can produce on demand:

    pip install pip-licenses && pip-licenses --format=markdown
    npm --prefix web ls --all --json


Runtime dependencies that carry a notice requirement
----------------------------------------------------

elkjs (Eclipse Public License 2.0)
    https://github.com/kieler/elkjs
    The graph layout engine behind the Explore canvas. EPL-2.0 code is bundled
    into the built frontend, so a distributed build carries it. The EPL asks
    that recipients be told where to obtain the source, which is the URL above.
    EPL-2.0: https://www.eclipse.org/legal/epl-2.0/

caniuse-lite (CC-BY-4.0)
    https://github.com/browserslist/caniuse-lite
    Browser support data used at build time by the bundler. CC-BY-4.0 asks for
    attribution, which this entry provides.
    CC-BY-4.0: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/


Example content that carries a notice requirement
---------------------------------------------------

CUAD dataset (CC-BY-4.0)
    https://www.atticusprojectai.org/cuad
    The 19 real contracts under examples/real_world/corpus/ are drawn from
    the Contract Understanding Atticus Dataset. CUAD's curation and labels
    are CC-BY-4.0. The underlying contracts are public SEC EDGAR filings.
    See examples/real_world/README.md for the full source note.
    CC-BY-4.0: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/


Everything else, in summary
---------------------------

Python: all dependencies are MIT, Apache-2.0, BSD, or MIT-CMU. No GPL or AGPL
code is used or distributed. PDF rendering uses pypdfium2 (BSD-3-Clause and
Apache-2.0) rather than PyMuPDF, because PyMuPDF is AGPL and cannot ship inside
an MIT-licensed project.

JavaScript: predominantly MIT, with ISC, Apache-2.0 and BSD-3-Clause packages,
plus the two entries named above.


Things pulled at runtime rather than distributed
------------------------------------------------

Azure Cosmos DB Emulator
    The local development stack pulls Microsoft's emulator image from a public
    registry. It is proprietary and governed by Microsoft's own EULA. It is not
    redistributed here, and it is not required in production, where a real
    Cosmos DB account takes its place.

OCR model weights
    Building the Docker image downloads ONNX weights derived from PaddleOCR
    (Apache-2.0). They are baked into the image you build, not into this
    repository.
